  {
    "episode_id": 90754,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90754.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 80,
    "turn_text": "So the honest truth is I should give all credit to my wife because they were short of coaches and my wife agreed to take some time coaching and and so I was really the assistant coach helping out it's a I have tremendous respect for coaches. It's really hard to coach your own kids. You don't want to play favorites. You've, of course, driving back in the car. I'm trying to be a dad, giving constructive criticism. And kids are sitting there saying, everybody else is coaching stopped when the game ended. I'd like my coaching is stopped when the game ends as well. And we also had, frankly, and this is You know, a little bit of a dab brag. I didn't coach my older daughter. She didn't play soccer. My younger daughter did. And she really got to be pretty good. But she was good because she was fast. And so, you know, I'd sit there watching her as a parent and saying, okay, your ball skills are not that good. You're not doing a very good job of moving to open space. But when you get the ball, you're so much quicker than the other kids that you just run down and you score. And at some point, they're all going to catch up to you. And so I'd sort of sit there and say, here's some things you should work on. And she'd say, I scored eight goals today. What do I need to work on? And that was the point when we realized we really need to get you in a league where you're going to be not the good kid on the team and knock you around a little bit and not be coached by your dad anymore. So I really enjoyed it for a couple of years, but I think she got as far as she could with my coaching talents.",
