  {
    "episode_id": 90754,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90754.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 93,
    "turn_text": "Yeah. Yeah. No, my, my daughters were bummed because I, I played, uh, This is kind of fun. There is a, there is a congressional soccer caucus, which I am proud to be a member of. I, I got to be a member because, you know, famously we do this congressional baseball game where, you know, they play at the national stadium and it's all members from against each other. There is also somewhat less famously a congressional soccer game. And that's somewhat less famous in no small part because you can, you know, you can be a, you know, a elderly member of Congress and, you know, and still swing a bat. But the average member of Congress cannot run up and down a soccer field. So the result is that we had, it's open to members and staff. And we had myself and Rick Larson were the only Democratic members playing. On the Republican side, it was, you know, Anthony Gonzalez, X football player. Dan Crenshaw, Garrett Graves, who went down with an injury midway through, which I tease him about to this day, that he couldn't hang with a big boy. And Darren Lehood, and we then had rounded out with a whole bunch of staffers who were all younger and in much better shape, including Maddie's sister, who played on our team. And then we still couldn't field a big enough team, so they brought in a bunch of ringers. We had some people from the World Cup team who were playing with us. And then the other team ended up getting Kobe Jones played for the Republican team.",
