    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 90754,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90754.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 103,
    "turn_text": "So, similar to you, I moved. So I was born in Ireland, lived in Indiana for a couple of years. grew up in New York. So we moved to Westchester County, New York when I was six and lived there until, you know, I went to college and I haven't lived back there since. But so, you know, living in New York from what would that have been, 77 to 89. And I, you know, what I knew with certainty as a kid was that I really, really didn't like the Yankees. I couldn't even tell you why I didn't like him. I liked maddingly. I didn't like Steinbrenner. I liked Winfield, and I hated that Steinbrenner was always mean that they've Winfield. And so for whatever kid logic, I became a Mets fan. And I think if your baseball team wins a World Series when you're 15, There's no better time to have, you know, the, you know, I could tell you to this day, they're starting line up and they're relievers. And, you know, I was at the game when Raphael Santana hit one of his two home runs of the season, little tiny short stuff. The end, you know, that whole 86 world series and everything that led into that and, you know, My brother teases me to this day that I thought the game was over and I couldn't bear to watch the ticker tape come down and actually went upstairs. And then my brother called and he's like, the bases are loaded. You got to get back out of here. And so those were sort of my touch points as a kids were just watching the Mets and that the unbelievable talent on that 85 to 88 team and how much of that was just totally destroyed by drugs. I mean, I think if there was a better peer hitter in baseball than Darryl Strawberry, I never saw him, but talk about like just an amazing talent that flamed out way too quick. And then of course, the next year, you know, the giants were my team and they won that Super Bowl, so that, you know, when Lawrence Taylor was in his prime and Pepper Johnson and just all those amazing offensive line they had. So that was sort of all like my, my youth was growing up with that. And then, you know, college in Vermont lived in Boston grad school in New Hampshire, you know, 20 years lived in New England. And I get up there and I'm like, all right. So I grew up rooting for other dogs that never win. And I don't like the Yankees, the Red Sox seem like super obvious here, right? And And then if you recall, Parcels came up to coach the Patriots and brought basically the entire 86 offensive line to be his assistant coaches. And so I was like, well, this is like super easy. I got teams that are never going to win, but I can start watching them. And then, of course, this huge New England dynasty started. And so it was nice to be there through my through that point in your life when, okay, you're not 15 anymore, but you've got an income and you're single. And you've got a job that is a 20 year old job. You don't have to work ridiculously long hours, you know, which means that you can always, you can always cut out of work and head to Fenway and buy a scalp ticket or whatever else. And, you know, and they took off and started winning. And then I moved to Chicago in 2007 and by rights, I really should be a die-hard Cub fan because that whole metaphor, like everything happened in the same way, right? Yeah. Theo Epstein packs up, leaves the Red Sox, brings some of the stars that old team over here and takes off, except that I moved here in 2007. And I think within two years of moving here, the Red Sox won, the Patriots won, the Bruins won, the Celtics won. And Rex Grossman lost 60 yards on pre-plays and the playoffs. Remember that? Oh, yeah. And I've got to be sure to say something. You're going to become a fan of all these teams. I'm like, give me just one reason, like",
