  {
    "episode_id": 90754,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90754.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 121,
    "turn_text": "So this is gonna sound weird and maybe over-intellectual, but I have found since getting in this new line of work that I have a new appreciation for what it must feel like to be LeBron James. And I say that because, you know, remember when LeBron first came up and he got all of this heat for traveling with his entourage and having, you know, his high school buddies on payroll. And there was always like, a not so subtle racial dog whistle about it. You're hanging out with your gang and all these guys and hoodies. We all know the code words that people use for that. And I'm not sure I was totally woke and self-aware when I heard that because I couldn't possibly imagine what it would be like to be LeBron James. And then you get elected. And all of a sudden find yourself, you know, okay, nowhere near that level of celebrity. But on television enough that when you walk around Palatine, you get recognized. And you become sort of keenly aware that when somebody comes and talks to you, are they talking to you because they are interested in a genuine human to human interaction? Or are they talking to you because You represent something to them that you have to maintain this fiction as one of my colleagues said, told me early on, when you get elected, you immediately become a vessel for a lot of people's dreams. And you have to keep in mind that when people cheer for you, they aren't cheering for the vessel. And you have to sort of figure out what is that thing that you represent to people? and have your B self aware enough to recognize that A, that thing is not necessarily you, but that B, that thing has a real value to people. You know, if you don't, I don't want to break the thing that holds your dreams in it. You know, so you have to kind of burnish and maintain this thing that's not totally you, but is hopefully close enough to who you are to be genuine. And the only people who can really tell you that are the people who you knew and trusted before you got to that degree of celebrity, which is why now I feel like I understand what drives a LeBron. But I mentioned all that in the context of your question, because in a Dick Durbin has this great, I don't know that he's ever said this publicly, but he's mentioned this to me a couple of times privately that he said, you know, If you gain a certain amount of political capital in this line of work, how do you use it? It's super easy if you get to a point where you can win an election by double digits, be safe a while. If you just sit there and coast, shame on you. But if you now have a platform where you can actually use that to move public opinion towards some you know, some more, more perfect tomorrow. That's a, there's almost an obligation to do that. And I think there's an analogy there to, you know, to athletes, you know, and you get, and look, I, you know, I got mad respect for Jordan as an athlete, even though he kept my nicks out of the Eastern Conference final",
